  "content": {
    "id": "p35-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Consider the hyperbola $y=\\frac1x$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p35-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Write the equation of $g(x)=2f(x)$. What dilation is this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$g(x)=\\frac2x$. Vertical dilation by factor $2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}g(x)&=2f(x)\\\\g(x)&=2\\left(\\frac1x\\right)\\\\g(x)&=\\frac2x\\end{align*}$$All $y$ coordinates are multiplied by $2$: a vertical dilation by factor $2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Write the equation of $h(x)=f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)$. What dilation is this?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(x)=\\frac2x$. Horizontal dilation by factor $2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}h(x)&=f\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\\\h(x)&=\\frac{1}{x/2}\\\\h(x)&=\\frac2x\\end{align*}$$Replacing $x$ with $\\frac{x}{2}$ multiplies all $x$ coordinates by $2$: a horizontal dilation by factor $2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q12-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "What do you notice about the answers to parts a and b?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both give the same equation $y=\\frac2x$. For $y=\\frac1x$, a vertical dilation by factor $k$ is equivalent to a horizontal dilation by factor $k$, where $k>0$.",
          "worked": "Both give the same equation $y=\\frac2x$. More generally, for $k>0$,$$\\begin{align*}f\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)&=\\frac{1}{x/k}\\\\f\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)&=\\frac{k}{x}\\\\f\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)&=kf(x)\\end{align*}$$Thus horizontal and vertical dilations by factor $k$ give the same graph.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
